last year china went from being the  biggest bitcoin mining country in the  world to going completely dark  chinese officials set off what became  known as the great mining migration when  they issued a statement vowing to crack  down on bitcoin mining and trading since  then the bitcoin mining industry has  relocated expanded and in some places  seized on innovative ways to harness and  use energy  [Music]  before we jump into how the industry has  evolved let's quickly talk about bitcoin  mining it isn't just about making money  machines around the world work to guess  a complex series of numbers in order to  validate a block of bitcoin transactions  the miner whose computer cracks the code  first wins some bitcoin those blocks of  transactions are recorded on the  blockchain ledger a giant public record  of all bitcoin transactions forever and  ever by design this process occurs  roughly every 10 minutes and it helps  prevent fraudulent activity  okay so it might be hard to wrap your  head around but what you need to know is  that the machines help secure and  maintain the bitcoin network and as a  reward the owners of those machines can  make a lot of money so why did beijing  ban mining  all those machines need electricity  ideally cheap electricity to maximize  profits and china has a lot of that  especially from coal before last year's  ban china had attracted so many miners  that the country accounted for nearly  half of all the computing power  dedicated to bitcoin mining that's known  in the crypto world as the hash rate all  of that energy use ran counter to  china's climate goals plus bitcoin was  seen as a threat to china's own digital  currency and its capital controls so  beijing cracked down and miners packed  up in late june footage from crypto  mining farms in china started popping up  on doin the chinese version of tiktok  showing the machines being dismantled  and moved  it was actually an  amazing year to be a bitcoin miner in  the us because uh basically half you  know half of your competition went away  with the swipe of a pen this is nick  hanson the ceo of luxor technologies a  bitcoin mining services company and that  thing sitting next to him that's a  bitcoin mining machine we were getting  calls from people that had machines on  boats on the way to the us they were  basically calling up and saying i need  somewhere to plug these in miners  ultimately want to go somewhere with  access to cheap energy and a predictable  regulatory environment so texas and  other u.s states quickly became hot  spots the u.s which was already adding  capacity before china's ban took the  mining crown and ran with it accounting  for more than a third of the global hash  rate kazakhstan and russia also gained  mining market share so what has this  meant for the bitcoin network some  believe there was an interesting  security ripple while china's move  initially rattled the crypto community  and tanked the price of bitcoin it  addressed a concern that the chinese  government could potentially exert some  control over the cryptocurrency because  so much mining was being done within its  borders in the months following china's  ban el salvador adopted bitcoin as a  national currency the u.s approved the  first bitcoin futures based etf and  professional investors surged into  cryptocurrencies at record rates just  this month fort worth texas became the  first u.s city to mine bitcoin with  public resources and fidelity announced  it would allow retirement savers to put  bitcoin in their 401ks while these moves  haven't explicitly been tied to  beijing's ban some in the crypto  community believe that because mining is  no longer concentrated in china some  officials and investors have become more  comfortable with bitcoin china's  crackdown has also caused ripple effects  for the energy industry the bitcoin  mining council a forum comprised of some  of the biggest players in the mining  industry released data indicating that  some notable things have happened since  the first quarter of last year after  china's ban there was a steep drop in  energy usage that still hasn't climbed  back to where it was even though the  hash rate the global computing power  dedicated to mining has reached new  highs the process of mining bitcoin has  become more efficient more hashes for  less energy and the percentage of  electricity coming from sustainable  sources to do that mining has jumped to  58  in the u.s where environmental standards  are higher and investors care about  reducing carbon emissions companies have  invested in a wide range of power  projects miners are partnering with  nuclear power plants that have excess  carbon free energy they're ramping up  businesses that harness the energy from  flare gas and they're repurposing old  fossil fuel factories to power their  machines with clean energy some  proponents of bitcoin even argue that  the mining industry is incentivizing the  growth of renewable energy  infrastructure if the wind is blowing  and the sun is shining but people don't  immediately need all of that energy  that's being produced that energy  doesn't get saved anywhere it doesn't  get banked or stored for another time  but miners are finding ways to use and  monetize that excess energy now you can  actually use bitcoin and  use that energy that would have  otherwise been either wasted or  discarded and  use it to make money and then reinvest  into more solar power more solar panels  more wind etc still as the u.s mining  industry grows it continues to face  backlash for contributing to climate  change pushing some companies to sign a  crypto climate accord nick's company  luxor is one of those companies in new  york a top mining state there's now a  political battle to restrict mining and  environmentalists have called on bitcoin  and other cryptocurrencies to lower  their energy use by abandoning the  current mining process known as the  proof-of-work method what do you say to  people who think  this proof-of-work method shouldn't  exist and you should just get rid of all  the computers proof-of-work is really  the only um method that we've seen uh  able to secure a trillion dollar network  anybody that's in bitcoin right now  thinks that bitcoin is still very much  in its infancy uh i would hate to uh  rock that boat uh especially when it's  so new changing the mining process would  require the approval of nearly all of  the miners and developers involved in  bitcoin something that's very unlikely  to happen at least anytime soon  while some investors and governments  around the world remain skeptical of  cryptocurrencies residents of turkey  have embraced them as their local  currency the turkish lira hovers at  record lows check out this video to  learn more about what's going on in  turkey thanks for watching and i'll see  you next time  you
 

